course: dictionary
lesson: 14277
title: The nominative: the doer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The nominative is a grammar word for the form of a noun or pronoun that does the action in a sentence. It is the subject, the one doing the action. «She kicked the ball.» She is nominative, because she is the one kicking.
explain: Check by asking, who is doing the action? In «The ball hit her», the ball is doing the hitting, so the ball is the subject. Her is not doing anything; she is the one being hit, so her is not nominative.
explain: In English, the nominative pronouns are I, he, she, we and they. The others, me, him, her, us and them, are not nominative. Some languages, such as Latin and German, change the endings of nouns to show this, so the word is useful when you learn them.
fact: Nominative means the form of a noun or pronoun used for the subject of a sentence.
fact: The subject is the one doing the action in a sentence.
fact: English nominative pronouns are I, he, she, we and they.
fact: Me, him, her, us and them are not nominative.
fact: «She and I went to the park.» Use the nominative when the pronoun is doing the action.
exercise: «___ and Ali kicked the ball.» Which pronoun fits the gap? | Me | Her | *She | Them
exercise: «Mum saw ___ at the park.» Which pronoun fits the gap, because we are the ones being seen? | I | They | We | *Us
exercise: «Ben gave the ball to him.» Which word is the subject, the one doing the action? | *Ben | To | Him | Ball
test: «___ are going to the beach today.» Which pronoun is nominative? | Us | Them | Me | *We
test: «The dog chased him.» Which word is the subject, the one doing the action? | *The dog | Chasing | Him | Chased
exam: Which sentence uses nominative pronouns correctly? | Him and me are friends | Me and him are friends | Us and they are friends | *He and I are friends
